1
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:04,470
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:05,170
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,710 --> 00:00:06,090
Hi everyone.

4
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:07,720
My name is Catherine.

5
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,310
What do you got, Catherine, for us today?

6
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:09,630
That's a good question.

7
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:12,250
Well, we've got a little bit of a Seinfeld situation.

8
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:27,830
So, for those of you who are not familiar with this American TV show, which, by the way, is one of my favorites, Seinfeld is a TV show, a comedy, about very awkward, very common situations that are taken to the extreme.

9
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:28,840
Right.

10
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:30,370
So, it's a very popular show.

11
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,040
It was around for like 10 years, I think.

12
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,040
Oh, yeah.

13
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,040
But it's not airing anymore.

14
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,590
Well, they're not making new episodes anymore.

15
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:38,390
No, you have to see them in reruns.

16
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:39,410
Reruns, right.

17
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:44,330
So, we have a situation that's very common and it was pretty funny on the show.

18
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:47,090
It's a guy trying to rent a car.

19
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:55,300
Okay, so he's probably at the airport or traveling and he's had a long flight and he wants to rent a car, but of course, there are some problems.

20
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,040
So, this is an elementary level lesson, by the way.

21
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:01,240
So keep that in mind as we listen to this dialogue one time.

22
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,240
Hi, I made a reservation for a mid-size vehicle.

23
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:11,680
The name is Jimmy Fox.

24
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:15,240
I'm sorry, we have no mid-size available at the moment.

25
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:16,560
I don't understand.

26
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:18,100
I made a reservation.

27
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,740
Do you have my reservation?

28
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:22,780
Yes, we do.

29
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:26,450
Unfortunately, we ran out of cars.

30
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:30,010
But the reservation keeps the car here.

31
00:01:30,850 --> 00:01:32,230
That's why you have the reservation.

32
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:34,730
I know why we have reservations.

33
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:37,190
I don't think you do.

34
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:39,250
If you did, I'd have a car.

35
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:41,770
See, you know how to take the reservation.

36
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:48,150
You just don't know how to hold the reservation and that's really the most important part of the reservation, the holding.

37
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:49,830
Anybody can just take them.

38
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,220
But we do have a compact or an SUV if you'd like.

39
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:55,260
Fine.

40
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:56,740
I'll take the compact.

41
00:01:57,840 --> 00:01:59,240
All right.

42
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:01,870
We have a blue Ford Focus for you, Mr. Fox.

43
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:03,210
Would you like insurance?

44
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,450
Yeah, you better give me the insurance because I'm going to beat the hell out of this car.

45
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,400
All right, great.

46
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,120
So this actually happens a lot.

47
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:21,280
You make a reservation and either they lose it or they run out of cars.

48
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:22,700
It's such a common situation.

49
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,780
And it's not just cars.

50
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,680
Sometimes this happens at restaurants too and it really makes me angry.

51
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:29,700
Yeah, that happens a lot.

52
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:31,760
You show up and they're like, oh, we don't have a reservation.

53
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:33,540
And you're just like...

54
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,560
Well, then you have to keep yourself in check.

55
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:36,840
You can't really get angry.

56
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:37,360
But this guy does.

57
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:46,380
So let's take a look at the language takeaway to find out what are some of the words we're using here to describe renting a car and what kind of car we're talking about.

58
00:02:48,710 --> 00:02:49,650
Language takeaway.

59
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:56,720
Well, as you mentioned, at rental places, they have different size cars, right?

60
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:57,560
Different size vehicles.

61
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,720
Which, by the way, costs different prices.

62
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:01,400
Right.

63
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,980
So let's take a look at the sizes that they mentioned in dialogue.

64
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:09,920
Starting with the cheapest one, the least expensive and the smallest, it's a compact vehicle.

65
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:12,160
All right, compact.

66
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,430
So compact actually means small.

67
00:03:14,630 --> 00:03:14,870
Small.

68
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:15,410
Right.

69
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:17,550
So compact car, you're going to hear very often.

70
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:20,610
Even if you're buying a car, let's think of an...

71
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:22,770
What's a compact car?

72
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:26,630
The VW Golf is a compact car, very small car.

73
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:32,560
So usually it's two doors or if it's a four door car, it's very small, not very long, right?

74
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:33,560
Right, exactly.

75
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,080
So maybe like a Ford Focus could be a small car, a compact.

76
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:39,630
Now moving up in size a little bit, we would have a midsize vehicle.

77
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,440
Okay, midsize vehicle is...

78
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,950
Well, it's a giveaway here because mid means middle.

79
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,870
So it's kind of in the middle between the small and the large.

80
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:50,530
And this is your average four door sedan car.

81
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:51,960
Okay.

82
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:57,110
So as an example, you would have a Ford Taurus or maybe a Toyota Camry.

83
00:03:57,730 --> 00:04:00,310
Yeah, Toyota Camry is maybe the most common car.

84
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:01,920
Yeah.

85
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:03,910
It's kind of expensive, but it's still midsize, right?

86
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:05,800
Exactly.

87
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:10,980
And now a bigger car and probably one of the most popular cars in the United States is an SUV.

88
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:12,440
Not anymore, Marco.

89
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:13,020
I know.

90
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:16,040
Yeah, the energy crisis and the...

91
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:16,500
Gas prices.

92
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,560
Well, and the economy as well.

93
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,000
People are not driving their SUVs anymore.

94
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,020
But SUV is an acronym.

95
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,720
That means it stands for three different words.

96
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:25,520
S meaning...

97
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:26,300
Sports.

98
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,120
U meaning...

99
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,120
Utility.

100
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:30,120
V...

101
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:31,120
Vehicle.

102
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:32,120
All right.

103
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:33,120
Thank you.

104
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:34,440
So SUV, sport utility vehicle, means it can go maybe off-road.

105
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:34,840
Right.

106
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:39,120
So an example of this would be a Ford Explorer or...

107
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:40,120
Jeep Cherokee.

108
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:42,520
A Jeep Cherokee could be an SUV as well.

109
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:43,960
That's right.

110
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:44,780
Those are three different types of cars.

111
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Now obviously there are many more like a van or a...

112
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:49,460
A limo.

113
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:51,060
A limo, like a limousine.

114
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:55,820
But yeah, these are the most common ones that you would rent at a car rental place.

115
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:57,720
Yeah, definitely.

116
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,340
But what we have to do here is make a reservation.

117
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,660
Now interesting, you make a reservation.

118
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:05,920
Yeah, you don't have it.

119
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:06,860
You don't do it.

120
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:07,520
You don't...

121
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:09,110
You make it.

122
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:10,740
Yeah, you make...

123
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,080
These are words that go together.

124
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:13,250
So we're going to keep them together.

125
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,480
I can call a restaurant.

126
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:16,870
So say for example, Marco works at a restaurant.

127
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:20,240
I say, hello, I'd like to make a reservation for tonight.

128
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:21,240
Oh, okay.

129
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:22,240
Perfect.

130
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:23,750
Now if you show up at the restaurant, you say...

131
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:25,270
Where's my table?

132
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,270
I made a reservation.

133
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:27,950
Right.

134
00:05:28,550 --> 00:05:32,110
And of course I throw a fit because there's no table for me.

135
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:36,280
So you make a reservation and in this case it was made before.

136
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:37,850
Okay, so you make a reservation.

137
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:40,850
Now why was this guy angry at the rental car place?

138
00:05:41,670 --> 00:05:46,970
Oh, long story short, the company ran out of cars.

139
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:51,170
Not all cars, just the car that he wanted, the mid-size vehicle.

140
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:53,010
So they ran out.

141
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,530
So that doesn't mean they're actually running somewhere.

142
00:05:55,670 --> 00:05:58,090
No, they're not putting on their running shoes and going for a jog.

143
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:05,680
Now in this case, to run out or in this case, we ran out means that they don't have any more.

144
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:06,680
You don't have any more.

145
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:07,680
Exactly.

146
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:12,720
So, I think it is a nice word for us to take a look at some other examples now.

147
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:13,000
Example one.

148
00:06:15,390 --> 00:06:16,630
We're going to run out of gasoline.

149
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:23,400
We need to find a gas station soon.

150
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:23,660
Example two.

151
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:28,010
Can you buy some milk on your way home?

152
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:29,510
We ran out last night.

153
00:06:32,670 --> 00:06:33,670
Example three.

154
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:36,540
My pen ran out of ink.

155
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:37,820
Can you lend me one, please?

156
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:49,300
Not a fun phrase, but an important one in case you're ever in a restaurant or in a hotel or in a place where you can purchase something or rent something and it's all gone.

157
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:50,800
All right.

158
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:53,460
So, I think we've taken a look at a lot of great words here.

159
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:55,840
So let's listen to this dialogue again.

160
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:59,980
We're going to slow it down just a little bit and then we'll come back and explain some phrases.

161
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,180
Hi, I made a reservation for a mid-size vehicle.

162
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:08,740
The name is Jimmy Fox.

163
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:10,200
I'm sorry.

164
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:13,280
We have no mid-size available at the moment.

165
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:14,600
I don't understand.

166
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:16,100
I made a reservation.

167
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:17,760
Do you have my reservation?

168
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:20,820
Yes, we do.

169
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,460
Unfortunately, we ran out of cars.

170
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,000
But the reservation keeps the car here.

171
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:30,260
That's why you have the reservation.

172
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:32,840
I know why we have reservations.

173
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:35,180
I don't think you do.

174
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:37,220
If you did, I'd have a car.

175
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:39,780
See, you know how to take the reservation.

176
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:42,860
You just don't know how to hold the reservation.

177
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:46,160
And that's really the most important part of the reservation, the holding.

178
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:47,840
Anybody can just take them.

179
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,200
But we do have a compact or an SUV if you'd like.

180
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:53,220
Fine.

181
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:54,740
I'll take the compact.

182
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:56,480
All right.

183
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:00,720
We have a blue Ford Focus for you, Mr. Fox.

184
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:01,240
Would you like insurance?

185
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:06,500
Yeah, you better give me the insurance because I'm going to beat the hell out of this car.

186
00:08:10,210 --> 00:08:11,110
And we're back.

187
00:08:11,130 --> 00:08:14,630
So, Marco, we've got a couple of phrases here that deserve some attention.

188
00:08:15,590 --> 00:08:17,770
So let's take a look at them in today's Fluency Builder.

189
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:19,940
Fluency Builder.

190
00:08:20,590 --> 00:08:29,630
So, the first phrase that I want to take a look at is when the agent says, I'm sorry, we have no mid-size available at the moment.

191
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:30,790
Okay.

192
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,090
So do they or do they not have any?

193
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:34,520
Yeah.

194
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:35,760
She says, we have no mid-size.

195
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:36,670
Okay.

196
00:08:36,690 --> 00:08:39,590
So another way to say this is, we haven't got any.

197
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:41,600
Or we don't have any.

198
00:08:41,710 --> 00:08:42,050
Okay.

199
00:08:42,050 --> 00:08:44,870
But in this case, you can just simplify it and say, we have no.

200
00:08:45,870 --> 00:08:46,870
Why wouldn't you say we have none?

201
00:08:47,260 --> 00:08:47,720
Good question.

202
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:49,160
Why?

203
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:50,460
Because we need a thing here.

204
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:52,080
We have no cars.

205
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:52,740
We have no cake.

206
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:53,820
We have no food.

207
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:57,440
So this is a very common phrase in English.

208
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:59,420
And remember to put the thing at the end.

209
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:04,440
So for example, can I say, I have no money?

210
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:05,100
Absolutely.

211
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:07,200
I'm so sorry, Marco, but I'm not going to lend you any.

212
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:07,920
I have no money.

213
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:09,920
Seriously, lend me some money.

214
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:10,840
So we have no.

215
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:17,720
This is interesting because this is not a structure that is usually learned through textbooks, right?

216
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:19,700
You usually say, I don't have or we haven't got.

217
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:20,400
Exactly.

218
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:24,480
This is very spoken because you would never say, I go no.

219
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:24,840
Right.

220
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:25,300
Exactly.

221
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:26,140
Right.

222
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:30,560
We're not knowing that we, that has an object, something that we have.

223
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:33,680
So here, I have no is very spoken, very common.

224
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:41,240
It's got another phrase that is also not taught so much in those textbooks that we were just mentioning.

225
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,320
So what's that phrase at the end?

226
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:43,560
Well, well.

227
00:09:44,940 --> 00:09:45,440
It's a threat.

228
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:45,840
It's a threat.

229
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:47,200
The guy is so angry.

230
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:52,240
He leans over the counter and he points his finger in the agent's face.

231
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:53,480
And what does he say?

232
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:56,220
He says, yeah, you better give me the insurance.

233
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:57,440
You better.

234
00:09:58,100 --> 00:09:58,320
You better.

235
00:09:59,100 --> 00:09:59,280
All right.

236
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:01,160
So you better than me.

237
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:02,000
No, no.

238
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,900
It's not like better, like something is good, right?

239
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:09,480
You'd better is like a suggestion, but a very strong one.

240
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:10,480
Okay.

241
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:10,600
Very strong.

242
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:12,480
This is a threat.

243
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:13,480
Yeah.

244
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:16,680
You better eat your dinner or I'm going to send you to your room.

245
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:17,680
Exactly.

246
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:22,760
So as you can see, it says, he says, you better, you better give me the insurance.

247
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:25,800
But the grammatically correct way would be.

248
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:27,360
You had better.

249
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:28,180
You had better.

250
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:30,620
So the contraction would be you'd better.

251
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:32,360
Exactly.

252
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:34,420
You'd, you had better do this.

253
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:37,760
So this is a, it's a hypothetical situation.

254
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:39,080
You had better do this.

255
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:41,240
If you don't do this, bad things will happen.

256
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:44,980
So remember that here we've got the grammatically correct form that you should write.

257
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:47,200
You'd better or the spoken form.

258
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:48,040
You better.

259
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:48,840
You better.

260
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:59,080
I think in this one, we should also listen to some other examples to get a better idea of how we can use this very strong suggestion.

261
00:10:59,080 --> 00:10:59,350
Example one.

262
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,620
You better go to the doctor before that cold gets any worse.

263
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:09,100
Example two.

264
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:12,230
You better watch out.

265
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:13,730
It's dangerous to go out this late.

266
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:18,360
Example three.

267
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:23,680
She better change her attitude or her boss is going to fire her.

268
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:27,720
So what is the threat?

269
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:31,400
You better give me insurance because what is he going to do?

270
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:34,940
He says, I'm going to beat the hell out of this car.

271
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:36,180
Okay, so this is future.

272
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:38,080
I'm going to, I'm gonna.

273
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:39,760
Right.

274
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,280
Beat the hell out of this car.

275
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:44,160
So this is very strong, right?

276
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:45,280
Yeah, it's very strong.

277
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:47,840
And he's saying he is going to destroy the car.

278
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:49,460
So beat the hell out of.

279
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:51,800
That means really just do damage to something.

280
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:54,200
So you can use it towards a person also.

281
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:57,000
It's like, I'm going to beat the hell out of you.

282
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,440
And I'll arrest you for violence.

283
00:11:59,180 --> 00:11:59,360
Right.

284
00:11:59,380 --> 00:12:00,820
So it's a very strong phrase.

285
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:02,320
It shows anger.

286
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:04,180
It shows that you are threatening somebody.

287
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:07,260
So it's not, obviously it's not polite.

288
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:13,260
And it might get you a broken nose in a bar if you ever say this to someone or about someone.

289
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:14,180
So be very careful.

290
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,560
Right.

291
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:16,840
But you can use it to describe a situation, right?

292
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:22,720
It's like, yeah, we went to a bar yesterday and Dan beat the hell out of the bartender.

293
00:12:23,530 --> 00:12:23,720
Right?

294
00:12:23,930 --> 00:12:24,330
Man.

295
00:12:24,770 --> 00:12:33,450
So instead of just saying Dan hit the bartender or Dan got into a fight, you're saying he hit him and he just.

296
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:36,170
And he kept hitting him and kept hitting him and kept hitting him.

297
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:38,110
So yeah, he beat the hell out of him.

298
00:12:38,290 --> 00:12:38,510
Right.

299
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:40,480
All right, great.

300
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:43,120
